{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A1etiajudxQKMQRWcvzaFr","type":"tool_result","content":"package scanner\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"path\"\n\t\"regexp\"\n\t\"strings\"\n)\n\nvar (\n\tllmRuleRe   = regexp.MustCompile(`^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}$`)\n\tllmPathRe   = regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z0-9._/@+-]+$`)\n\tllmSymbolRe = regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_./-]{0,127}$`)\n)\n\n// ParseLLMFingerprint splits an LLM scanner fingerprint, \"rule:path\" or\n// \"rule:path:symbol\", and reports whether it is well formed. The path must\n// be a clean, relative, repo-style path without \"..\" segments.\nfunc ParseLLMFingerprint(fp string) (rule, p, symbol string, ok bool) {\n\tif len(fp) > MaxFingerprint {\n\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t}\n\tparts := strings.Split(fp, \":\")\n\tif len(parts) < 2 || len(parts) > 3 {\n\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t}\n\trule, p = parts[0], parts[1]\n\tif len(parts) == 3 {\n\t\tsymbol = parts[2]\n\t\tif !llmSymbolRe.MatchString(symbol) {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif !llmRuleRe.MatchString(rule) || !llmPathRe.MatchString(p) {\n\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t}\n\tif p == \".\" || strings.HasPrefix(p, \"/\") || path.Clean(p) != p {\n\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t}\n\tfor _, seg := range strings.Split(p, \"/\") {\n\t\tif seg == \"..\" {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn rule, p, symbol, true\n}\n61:\tb.WriteString(\"- `scanners/trufflehog` is included. It files leaked-credential issues for a human (never for an agent) and needs a TruffleHog binary (AGPL-3.0; it is installed by the role's Dockerfile or taken from the host, and is not in the ard image). To opt out, delete `.ard/scanners/trufflehog/` and its `scanners/trufflehog` line under `overrides:` in `.ard/config.yaml` before merging.\\n\")\n62:\tb.WriteString(\"- `scanners/trufflehog-history` is included. It scans the full git history weekly for leaked credentials, with the same fingerprints as `scanners/trufflehog`, and its issues never autoclose. It reads the controller's clone through a read-only mount and needs the same TruffleHog binary. To opt out, delete `.ard/scanners/trufflehog-history/` and its `scanners/trufflehog-history` line under `overrides:` in `.ard/config.yaml` before merging.\\n\")\ndocs/layout.md:26:internal/scanner/           scanner pipeline: findings, markers, reconcile, diff, group, ExecEngine, AgentEngine (prompt.txt scanners), Engines, the pre-filing guard, the scanners Loop, rebuild\ndocs/layout.md:40:internal/roles/             starter .ard/ tree (config.yaml, role dirs) embedded for ard init (scanners/cve, scanners/docs, scanners/error-codes, scanners/lint, scanners/missing-tests, scanners/trufflehog, scanners/trufflehog-history, triage/lead, triage/engineer, workers/engineer, reviewers/eng-review, reviewers/license-review, reviewers/security-review)\ndocs/site/roles/scanners.md:5:A scanner finds problems (CVEs, lint, missing tests, docs gaps, secrets) and files issues. It is a directory, `.ard/scanners/<name>/`, holding either an executable `scan` that prints findings as JSON lines or a `prompt.txt` for an LLM scanner. The controller does all dedupe, grouping, filing and closing, so both kinds share one pipeline and no scanner holds a GitHub token.\ndocs/site/roles/scanners.md:24:Set these under `overrides` for `scanners/<name>` (see [Configuration](../configuration.md)): `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `route`, `timeout`. The global `intervals.scanners` and `global_max_open` apply to all scanners.\n260:**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; history is scanned by `scanners/trufflehog-history`. The forbidden flags (`--since-commit`, `--max-depth`, `--detector-timeout`, `--archive-timeout`, `--filter-unverified`, `--filter-entropy`, `--results`) are never passed, because a partial or load-dependent result would autoclose live findings. Caveats: (1) a secret gone from HEAD is not revoked; autoclose closes the issue as completed, so the issue body tells the human to rotate first. (2) The fingerprint hash is unsalted, an offline oracle for a low-entropy value that is already in the repo at HEAD; accepted. (3) Issues on a public repo show `file:line`. (4) At `max_open` the scanner is skipped, so new leaks go unseen until a human resolves one; scanner failure streaks are posted on the status issue (#221). (5) Any future change that commits scanner logs must exclude this scanner. (6) On a public repo with GitHub secret scanning off, this scanner is the only detection channel, and it cannot stop a push. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile (or a host install) and is not in the published `ard` image or the root `Dockerfile`. Host and Docker integration tests (`go test -tags integration -run Trufflehog ./internal/roles/`, with `ARD_TRUFFLEHOG_BIN` at the Dockerfile's pinned version) prove the role against a real binary and image.\n262:**The trufflehog-history scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog-history`) runs `trufflehog git file://$ARD_HISTORY --bare` with the same flags plus no `--since-commit`, `--max-depth` or `--branch`, over every fetched ref, with the same hash salt, so a value has the same fingerprint as at HEAD. A scanner role asks for history by holding an empty file named `history` in its directory (`Role.ReadsHistory`; there is no config key, so an override cannot grant it). The executor then sets `Job.History` (scanner stage only) and mounts the controller's bare clone read-only at `/history` in Docker (host mode uses the clone path) and sets `ARD_HISTORY`. The mount includes unmerged PR branches. Commit, author and timestamp are dropped; only the detector, file, line and hash leave the scan. It never autocloses, because history does not go away. A Kubernetes executor must provide a read-only copy at the same place.\n504:  scanners/trufflehog:      { every: 24h, max_open: 5, group_size: 1, autoclose: true, route: needs-human, timeout: 30m }\n505:  scanners/trufflehog-history: { every: 7d, max_open: 5, group_size: 1, autoclose: false, route: needs-human, timeout: 60m }\n524:**`ard init` creates and upgrades `.ard/` (decided, #27; revised, #303).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/lts`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lts, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), then brings `.ard/` up to them. By default it writes the **local working tree** for a human to commit; with `--pr` it commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR, as before. `--pr` works with a GitHub App, `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` or the `gh` CLI fallback (the same sources as `ard serve`, #458); with a token the PR is authored by the token's user, and the PR body's follow-up section is worded for either kind. Either way a human commits or merges: ard never writes `main`, and `scan` and `aid` are written `100755`. **Upgrades** are tracked in `.ard/ard-init.lock`, a YAML file holding the sha256 of every file init created or updated; init adds or refreshes a file's entry whenever it writes that file. The user may edit the lock; ard uses it only to decide whether a file changed since init wrote it. Per file: absent, create it; identical to the new starter, leave it; hash equal to its lock entry, update it; anything else (including no lock or no entry) counts as edited and is never overwritten: init lists it with the starter's diff. A file the starter dropped is listed, never deleted, and init writes nothing outside `.ard/`. The three repo context templates (`.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md`) are create-if-absent: written only when missing, never treated as an upgrade and never overwritten; the next steps tell an adopter with an existing config to add their paths to `merge.protected_paths`. In local mode init refuses while `.ard/` has uncommitted changes; in `--pr` mode it refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits. If nothing differs it prints `nothing to change`. Init runs the shared permission check (#302: repo reachable, App installed, the permissions ard needs) and reports it; a failed check warns in local mode and fails in `--pr` mode. It no longer syncs labels: that moved to `ard setup`. With `--dry-run` it prints the plan and writes nothing (local mode) or logs the commit, push and PR to the ledger (`--pr`). It ends by naming the next steps: commit, merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit** (never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history in `--pr` mode; **Rebase and merge** is also safe, #85), then `ard setup`. When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed.\n73:\t\"max_budget_usd\": true, \"budget_usd_per_day\": true, \"tokens_per_day\": true, \"network\": true,\n331:\t\tcase \"max_budget_usd\", \"budget_usd_per_day\":\n341:\t\t\tif field == \"max_budget_usd\" {\n409:\t\t\t*errs = append(*errs, &Error{File: cfgPath, Key: keyRef + \".max_budget_usd\", Msg: \"an LLM scanner needs max_budget_usd greater than 0\"})\n411:\t\trole.MaxBudgetUSD = *ov.maxBudgetUSD","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca5ddae6-22d1-4b15-a7d7-5ce436945bda","uuid":"4a06b061-6392-4a11-81fd-cf42b71e2b8e","timestamp":"2026-11-28T07:06:06.553Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"package scanner\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"path\"\n\t\"regexp\"\n\t\"strings\"\n)\n\nvar (\n\tllmRuleRe   = regexp.MustCompile(`^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}$`)\n\tllmPathRe   = regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z0-9._/@+-]+$`)\n\tllmSymbolRe = regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_./-]{0,127}$`)\n)\n\n// ParseLLMFingerprint splits an LLM scanner fingerprint, \"rule:path\" or\n// \"rule:path:symbol\", and reports whether it is well formed. The path must\n// be a clean, relative, repo-style path without \"..\" segments.\nfunc ParseLLMFingerprint(fp string) (rule, p, symbol string, ok bool) {\n\tif len(fp) > MaxFingerprint {\n\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\", false\n\t}\n\tparts := strings.Split(fp, \":\")\n\tif len(parts) < 2 || len(parts) > 3 {\n\t\treturn \"\", \"\", \"\